--- import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro"; import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro"; import "../../styles/standards-poster.css"; import "../../styles/extended.css"; import metadata from "../../data/standards/japangaap/asbj20.json"; --- <StandardPageLayout code={metadata.code} standardType={metadata.framework} title={metadata.title} description={metadata.description} issued={metadata.issued} latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment} status={metadata.status} summary={metadata.summary} bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice} > <Fragment slot="toc"> <PageToc items={[{"id":"s1","label":"Conceptual Framework Linkage"},{"id":"s2","label":"Scope — Investment and Rental Property"},{"id":"s3","label":"Balance Sheet Measurement — Cost Less Depreciation, Always"},{"id":"s4","label":"THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Fair Value Recognition Option, Unlike IAS 40"},{"id":"s5","label":"What This Statement Actually Requires — Note Disclosure of Fair Value"},{"id":"s6","label":"Determining Fair Value for Disclosure Purposes"},{"id":"s7","label":"Worked Example — Cost-Based Balance Sheet Measurement With Fair Value Disclosure"},{"id":"s8","label":"Worked Example — a Significant Fair Value Gap"},{"id":"s9","label":"Disclosure Requirements"},{"id":"s10","label":"Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates"},{"id":"s11","label":"Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP"},{"id":"s12","label":"Statement Mapping"},{"id":"s13","label":"Related Standards"},{"id":"s14","label":"Glossary"}]} /> </Fragment> <div class="wrap"> <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Fair value as supplementary information, not a Balance Sheet measurement basis</div><div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 20 addresses HOW companies should DISCLOSE information about the FAIR VALUE of INVESTMENT and RENTAL property — property held for RENTAL INCOME or CAPITAL APPRECIATION, rather than for OWNER-OCCUPIED business use. CRITICALLY, this Statement is a <strong>DISCLOSURE-ONLY</strong> standard — it does NOT change how such property is MEASURED on the face of the Balance Sheet, which remains at COST under Japan's general fixed asset framework.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Investment and Rental Property</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Property held for rental income or capital appreciation</div><div class="principle">This Statement applies to property held for the purpose of EARNING RENTAL income or CAPITAL APPRECIATION (rather than for use in the entity's OWN business operations, production, or administration) — commonly referred to as "investment property" or "rental property" (賃貸等不動産).</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Balance Sheet Measurement — Cost Less Depreciation, Always</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">No fair value remeasurement option exists under J-GAAP</div><div class="text-supporting">Under J-GAAP's GENERAL fixed asset framework, investment and rental property is measured at COST, LESS accumulated depreciation and any IMPAIRMENT — EXACTLY the same measurement basis applied to ORDINARY, owner-occupied tangible fixed assets. There is NO ELECTION or OPTION under J-GAAP to instead measure such property at FAIR VALUE on the face of the Balance Sheet, REGARDLESS of how significantly fair value may have diverged from the property's carrying cost.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>THE KEY DIVERGENCE — No Fair Value Recognition Option, Unlike IAS 40</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A "disclose but don't recognise" approach, distinct from IFRS's genuine measurement choice</div><div class="text-supporting">INTERNATIONALLY, IAS 40 (Investment Property) offers reporting entities a GENUINE ACCOUNTING POLICY CHOICE: measure investment property EITHER at COST (with depreciation, similar to J-GAAP), OR at FAIR VALUE, with CHANGES in fair value recognised DIRECTLY through PROFIT OR LOSS each period — a SUBSTANTIVE measurement OPTION with REAL Balance Sheet and Income Statement consequences. J-GAAP provides <strong>NO SUCH OPTION</strong> — cost-based measurement is MANDATORY for all investment/rental property, and FAIR VALUE information is provided ONLY as SUPPLEMENTARY NOTE DISCLOSURE (Section 5), with NO effect whatsoever on the RECOGNISED Balance Sheet carrying amount or the Statement of Income. This is a genuinely NOTABLE, well-recognised divergence from IAS 40's fair value model.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>What This Statement Actually Requires — Note Disclosure of Fair Value</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Fair value information provided alongside, not instead of, the cost-based figures</div><div class="text-supporting">For MATERIAL holdings of investment/rental property, companies must DISCLOSE (in the notes) the FAIR VALUE of such property AS AT the reporting date — ALONGSIDE, but NOT replacing, the COST-based carrying amount shown on the face of the Balance Sheet — giving readers ADDITIONAL insight into the property's CURRENT economic value WITHOUT altering the PRIMARY, cost-based financial statement figures themselves.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Determining Fair Value for Disclosure Purposes</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Generally based on appraisal value or a reasonably estimated market price</div><div class="text-supporting">Fair value for DISCLOSURE purposes is generally determined based on a REAL ESTATE APPRAISAL (鑑定評価) or another REASONABLY ESTIMATED market price — reflecting a genuine, CURRENT market-based value estimate, even though this figure NEVER flows into the RECOGNISED Balance Sheet carrying amount itself.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Cost-Based Balance Sheet Measurement With Fair Value Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Two figures, only one of which appears on the Balance Sheet</div> <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: A rental office building</div><div class="wk-body">A company holds a rental office building at a COST of ¥800,000,000, LESS accumulated depreciation of ¥150,000,000 (carrying amount: ¥650,000,000). An independent appraisal estimates the property's CURRENT fair value at ¥1,100,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The BALANCE SHEET continues to show the property at its <strong>¥650,000,000 COST-BASED carrying amount</strong> — UNCHANGED by the appraisal. SEPARATELY, in the NOTES, the company discloses the property's <strong>¥1,100,000,000 FAIR VALUE</strong> — purely SUPPLEMENTARY information, with NO effect on the recognised Balance Sheet figure or the Statement of Income.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — a Significant Fair Value Gap</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A large divergence, still with no Balance Sheet consequence</div> <div class="worked"><div class="wk-title">Example: Property appreciation over a long holding period</div><div class="wk-body">A company has held a piece of land for rental purposes for 30 years, originally acquired for ¥200,000,000 (land is NOT depreciated under J-GAAP). Current fair value, given significant appreciation, is estimated at ¥900,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">The Balance Sheet CONTINUES to show the land at its ORIGINAL <strong>¥200,000,000 COST</strong> — EVEN THOUGH its fair value has risen to ¥900,000,000 — with ONLY the ¥900,000,000 figure disclosed SEPARATELY in the notes. UNLIKE an entity electing the IAS 40 fair value model internationally, NO ¥700,000,000 unrealised gain is EVER recognised through profit or loss under J-GAAP, however LARGE the divergence becomes.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 20 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Carrying amount and fair value of investment/rental property, and the basis for determining fair value</span><span>Movement in carrying amount during the period</span><span>Rental income and related profit/loss from investment/rental property</span></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div> <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether property qualifies as investment/rental property versus owner-occupied use</span><span>Whether the fair value disclosure is material enough to require separate presentation</span></div></div> <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value determination, generally based on independent appraisal</span></div></div></div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison across all three major frameworks</div><div class="th cols-1-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 20)</span><span>IFRS (IAS 40) / US GAAP</span></div> <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Fair value Balance Sheet option</span><span>NO — cost-based measurement is mandatory</span><span>IFRS: YES — a genuine fair value model option. US GAAP: NO equivalent fair value option, generally cost-based like J-GAAP</span></div> <div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Fair value note disclosure</span><span>REQUIRED for material holdings</span><span>IFRS: required if the cost model is elected. US GAAP: not a general requirement, though real estate entities may provide supplemental metrics (e.g. FFO — see the companion ASC 974 poster)</span> <div class="note">On this SPECIFIC point, J-GAAP and US GAAP are genuinely MORE ALIGNED with EACH OTHER (both mandating cost-based measurement) than either is with IFRS's fair value OPTION under IAS 40 — a notable case where the "convergence with IFRS" pattern seen elsewhere in J-GAAP does NOT hold, and Japan instead sits CLOSER to the US GAAP position.</div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every investment/rental property event lands</div><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div> <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Cost-based carrying amount</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Cost less depreciation and impairment</span></div> <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Fair value disclosure</span><span>Notes only</span><span>No effect on recognised Balance Sheet or Statement of Income figures</span> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div><div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div> <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 10</span><span>Supplies the general cost-based measurement framework applied to investment/rental property</span></div> </div> </div> </div><div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid"> <div class="card cn span2"> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Investment/rental property (賃貸等不動産)</div><div class="glossary-def">Property held for rental income or capital appreciation, measured at cost on the Balance Sheet with fair value disclosed only in the notes.</div></div> <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Real estate appraisal (鑑定評価)</div><div class="glossary-def">An independent professional valuation commonly used to determine the fair value disclosed for investment/rental property under this Statement.</div></div> </div> </div><div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書)</span><span class="litem"><span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</span></div></div> </div> </StandardPageLayout> 